{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\n**Early life and succession**\n\nRaymond III was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father was assassinated. His early reign was marked by instability and conflict with the Zengid dynasty led by Nur ad-Din. In 1164 he was captured at the Battle of Harim and spent nearly a decade imprisoned. Upon his release in 1174 he returned to Tripoli but faced challenges consolidating power.\n\n**Political career and alliances**\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He acted as a regent for the young King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem from 1174 to 1177. During this time he formed an alliance with Bohemond III of Antioch to counter the influence of the faction led by Miles of Plancy and later Raymond of Tiberias. Raymond III supported the policy of coexistence with the Muslim powers including Saladin which put him at odds with more hardline factions among the Crusaders.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nIn 1177 Raymond III played a crucial role in the victory at the Battle of Montgisard where the Crusader forces under Baldwin IV defeated Saladin. He continued to serve as regent until Baldwin IV came of age in 1177. Raymond III also mediated disputes between the Crusader states and worked to maintain the fragile alliances necessary to defend against Muslim threats. His diplomacy was instrumental in securing temporary truces with Saladin that provided respite for the Crusader states.\n\n**Later years and death**\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nIn his later years Raymond III faced increasing pressure from both internal and external enemies. In 1187 he led the Crusader forces at the disastrous Battle of Hattin where Saladin inflicted a crushing defeat. Raymond III managed to escape but his reputation suffered. He died in 1187 without a male heir which led to the county of Tripoli passing to his distant relative Raymond IV of Toulouse. His death marked the end of an era for the Crusader states as the loss of Tripoli followed soon after. Raymond III is remembered as a skilled diplomat and leader who navigated the complex political landscape of the Crusader states during a period of great turmoil.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.22343038022518158}, {"token": "Summary", "logprob": -1.5471279621124268}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00033778208307921886}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.1223791316151619}, {"token": " Wikipedia", "logprob": -6.508615479106084e-05}, {"token": " page", "logprob": -0.5759861469268799}, {"token": " \"", "logprob": -0.09582949429750443}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -9.77468371274881e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.529942543740617e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00011085849109804258}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.011138285510241985}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.362964682513848e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": "\"", "logprob": -0.0008912881603464484}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -5.531158240046352e-05}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -3.0571413040161133}, {"token": "Early", "logprob": -0.45215311646461487}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.314470112323761}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.06387260556221008}, {"token": " succession", "logprob": -0.7086120247840881}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.9910473823547363}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004227867349982262}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00010156115604331717}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.1732504814863205}, {"token": " born", "logprob": -0.3259645998477936}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.9441131949424744}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.07074479013681412}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.37